  "question": {
    "text": "The correct statement about the magnetic properties of [Fe(CN)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3-</sup> and [FeF<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3-</sup> is : (Z = 26).",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "tojML",
      "text": "Both are paramagnetic.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "XfdAJ",
      "text": "Both are diamagnetic.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "lGQ08",
      "text": "[Fe(CN)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3- &nbsp;</sup>is paramagnetic,<br /><br />&nbsp;[FeF<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3- </sup>is diamagnetic.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "rzFvn",
      "text": "[Fe(CN)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3- &nbsp;</sup>is diamagnetic,<br /><br />[FeF<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3-&nbsp;</sup>is paramagnetic.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "In both complexes Fe is in +3 oxidation state.<br />F<sup>-</sup> ion &nbsp;is weak field ligand&nbsp;so no pairing of electrons in inner 3d-orbitals &amp; outer orbital complex. sp<sup>3</sup>d<sup>2</sup>&nbsp;hybridisation is formed.<br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/EM0025961_AnsExp_1.jpg\" style=\"width: 472px; height: 104px;\" /><br />have 5 unpaired electrons, so paramagnetic<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#956;</mi><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>FeF</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>35</mn><mo>&#160;</mo></msqrt><mtext>B</mtext><mo>.</mo><mtext>M</mtext></math><br />​​<br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/EM0025961_AnsExp_2.jpg\" style=\"opacity: 0.9; width: 369px; height: 87px;\" /><br />One unpaired Electron, CN<sup>-</sup> ion strong ligand field. So will pair&nbsp;the d-orbital electrons &amp; form inner orbital hybridization d<sup>2</sup>sp<sup>3</sup>&nbsp;.<br /><br /><math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>&#956;</mtext> <msup> <mrow> <mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mtext>Fe</mtext> <msub> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mtext>CN</mtext> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mn>6</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mo>-</mo> </mrow> </msup> </msub> <mo>=</mo> <msqrt> <mn>1</mn> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> <mo>+</mo> <mn>2</mn>&#160; &#160;&#160;</mrow></mfenced></msqrt></mrow></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(1 unpaired e<sup>-</sup>&nbsp;so para magnetic)<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo> <msqrt> <mn>3</mn> </msqrt> <mo>B.M</mo> </mrow> </math>.&nbsp;<br />&nbsp;",
